<Card title="About the Gracie Combatives Test">
  <p>
    The Gracie Combatives Proficiency Drills (GCPDs) are a standardized test to assess your skill in the 36 core techniques[cite: 2741]. Passing signifies you understand the core self-defense principles[cite: 2738].
  </p>
  <p>
    The test consists of <strong>five drills</strong>[cite: 2740]:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Drills 1-4:</strong> Cover techniques from Mount, Guard, Side Mount, and Standing positions. You must demonstrate all variations in the specific order within a <strong>5-minute time limit</strong> for each drill[cite: 2742, 2743].</li>
    <li><strong>Drill 5:</strong> A 5-minute <strong>Freestyle Fight Simulation</strong> testing your ability to execute techniques based on your partner's unpredictable actions, focusing on <strong>reflexes</strong>[cite: 2744, 2755].</li>
  </ul>
</Card>

<Card title="Evaluation Criteria" className="mt-4">
  <p>
    You start with 100 points. A score of <strong>90 or higher</strong> is required to pass[cite: 2746]. Deductions (1 point each) are given for significant errors[cite: 2745]. Evaluators focus on three key areas:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Details:</strong> Do you understand the "big picture" concept of the technique? [cite: 2747, 2749]</li>
    <li><strong>Conviction:</strong> Do you apply the technique with belief and effectiveness, even against moderate resistance? [cite: 2750, 2752, 2753]</li>
    <li><strong>Reflexes:</strong> How quickly and appropriately do you respond to your partner's actions (indicators), especially in Drill 5? [cite: 2754, 2755]</li>
  </ol>
</Card>

<Card title="Common Mistakes to Avoid" className="mt-4">
  <ul>
    <li>Failing to Stand Up in Base consistently[cite: 2767].</li>
    <li>Poor Head/Posture Control during defense[cite: 2768, 2769].</li>
    <li>Having a partner ("Bad Guy") who doesn't provide realistic indicators[cite: 2770].</li>
    <li>Skipping any required slice or variation during the drills[cite: 2771].</li>
  </ul>
</Card>